[Music]  this morning our series pushing the  limits takes us to a school that's  empowering young people of color who  have sky-high ambitions the new  york-based red tail flight academy is  training black men and women for careers  in aviation the program is inspired by  tuskegee airmen the country's first  black military pilots michael george  spoke with several students clocking  flight hours as they prepare to become  commercial pilots  the freedom of soaring through the skies  that's been a dream for 21 year old trey  jackson his entire life ever since like  two years old i saw the movie top gun i  feel the need  for speed  seeing tom cruise in the f-14s  that was my whole entire motivation to  look into the world of aviation but  becoming a pilot wouldn't be easy  training alone can cost around a hundred  thousand dollars  then jackson got a call he never  expected he'd been chosen to join a  program looking for a new generation of  young black pilots i literally prayed  for something like exactly like this  program the program called red tail  flight academy is inspired by the  legendary tuskegee airmen pilots trained  the tuskegee way go on to earn high  praise the first black military pilots  who battled both nazis during world war  ii and racism back home  jackson and fellow aspiring pilot maya  coley are a part of the academy's first  class  what did it feel like when you heard you  were chosen when you were selected for  this program  i cried  it was unbelievable for me because  a lot of people who look like me they  don't know where to start or they don't  know how to start because it is such a  financial burden but 80 years after the  tuskegee airmen first took flight  aviators like jackson and coley are  still rare black aircraft pilots and  engineers make up less than four percent  of those employed in the industry i  think we're making a change glenn  frazier founder of red tail made it his  mission to diversify the skies there is  a problem with awareness within the  inner city in the urban areas to know  that this particular career  is a possibility the simulators are  really a critical component board member  ann palmer says the young pilots in  training now carry the legacy of the  tuskegee airmen a legacy started by  aviators like her father one of the  original airmen my father was second  lieutenant augustus l palmer an  extraordinary father but an  extraordinary man this is personal for  you very very there's nothing greater  than to see  what  your ancestors stood for  actually honored and perpetuated  what do you think your dad would say  seeing them take to the sky  straighten up fly right  get it done introduce another notch of  flaps jackson and coley are now living a  dream they once thought was out of their  reach it's always been that intimidation  of  for me black women can't fly or black  women won't be able to take on this  challenge  and that was motivation for me what does  it feel like now to take off  oh my gosh  honestly it's been like the most like  amazing experience oh sorry i'm teared  up that's all right yeah um  i was telling my family i wanted to be a  pilot and then actually finally being  able to and actually take off and  actually holding my pipe's license it  was a great experience  [Applause]  jackson now has more than 150 flight  hours you're about the same age as the  tuskegee airmen were when they flew do  you ever think about that think about  that all the time it's a lot of  motivation to wake up in the morning  jackson and coley both hope to become  commercial pilots when they graduate  this spring and they'll always remember  the shoulders they're standing on  trey what do you think the tuskegee  airmen would say about  you guys  i think that would definitely say that  we are following in their footsteps and  um  really trying to just push  the  just the bar even higher each and every  time  for cbs mornings michael george new  windsor new york  i love his passion for it that it drives  him to tears just thinking about what it  represents how far he's come and you  know really talk about standing on the  shoulders of ancestors yeah to be able  to do  and what that plan looked very little to  me that was the boy they started a  little bit he loved it they started the  little ones and then they did the big  ones yeah aviation is a possibility yeah  young black kids yeah that's awesome  great advertisement for flying just that  smile yes

